From: marym@Finesse.COM (Mary Morris)
Date: Thu, 23 Jun 1994 11:47:51 +0800
Subject: [#94-6-104] [#94-6-88] digital cash and free banking

tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns) writes:

>>From: marym@Finesse.COM (Mary Morris)
>>At present, I get my paycheck deposited to my bank account electronically.
>>I tend to use a debit card for most general transactions. [...]
>>My bills are paid through Check-Free, a service that I pay for.
>>Very little paper (cash or check) is ever used by me.
>
>Most of the responses (listing various advantages and conveniences of
>digicash) to my questions ignored this option. This is the real alternative
>to digicash (call it digicheck). The question is anonymous versus
>data-trail electronic transfers, not anonymous tranfers versus nothing.
>Will we be forced to choose between leaving a data trail at every
>transaction, like Mary, or sticking with old-fashioned greenbacks? Are
>there enough legitimate users and uses of anonymity for it to get a
>foothold or is the black market our best hope for privacy?

About four or five years ago, I worked for a place that processed 
bank data. This wasn't the average transaction processing. This place
took all of a bank's data and merged it together to identify
what accounts a person had, how money flowed between them and out 
of them. This was then merged with several demographic and lifestyle
databases. The end result was a very good profile of what a "household"
(that was their base unit) did with its money. I worked in the operations
area verifying the fuzzy logic matches that were made to determine
who was who and what they did. 

As far as I know, this info stayed inside of the bank. It wasn't information
that was for sale. With the increasing value of information, I don't
expect this *ethical stance* to remain. This was/is a necessary
part of what the marketing and advertising needs to know to get their
financial support.

Many banks profile people now. I know that as long as I have my money
in a bank, I am probably subject to this without my knowledge or 
approval. I have no way to identify who uses this and who doesn't
now.

If you really want your digicash to be anonymous, you will probably 
end up paying an additional fee equal to what the institution would
get for selling that info on the open market.

Considering the Georgia Tech study, WHOIS database, and demographic
collection currently entertained by GNN and some WWW site dealing
in publishing magazine excerpts, there is enough information for
basic profiling without specific transaction information. If 
transaction information is combined, the clarity is significantly
improved. 

Anyway, after all of this rambling, the financial institutions currently
*own* your financial data. (Possetion is nine tenth's of the law).
Most people don't know this. I don't really expect a digibank
to have any different perspective on what it *owns*. The transfers
can be as anonymous as hell, but you are still marked and known.

Mary



