From: levy@lenny.haskins.yale.edu
Date: Thu, 23 Jun 1994 18:54:46 -0400
Subject: [#94-6-106] SCI: Chaos, Complexity, and Reductionism

About emergence, Derek Zahn wrote:

>  * It requires an observer interested in "talking about" the phenomena.
>    The pure compression view may not require this, but talk about
>    the movement of a tornado is only approximate talk, and it's not
>    clear to me how we can decide a priori which are the "important"
>    parts to be approximated.

I think that there's a thoroughly quantitative and objective way out of the 
subjective view of emergence, if you look at correlations across various
distances.  In general, emergence manifests itself as sudden, large changes
in the distance over which a system exhibits its greatest correlations.

For example, in the famous Benard convection, which occurs when
you heat a thin layer of water between two sheets of glass, you start out
with very short-distance correlations (on the order of a few water molecules'
diameters) among adjacent molecules, whose movement is essentially random.
As you increase the temperature, the correlations suddenly jump to several 
millimeters' distance, as large convection cells begin to form, and huge
numbers of molecules move around and around together.

In the other direction, you have the change from laminar to turbulent flow,
as when a rapidly-moving stream of water hits a rock.  Then, the long-distance
correlation between the molecules suddenly dissappears, as they swirl and
tumble on the downstream side of the rock.

This sudden, objective hyper-shift in correlations corresponds quite well to 
our subjective sense of surprise as we observe the system in question.  One 
physicist, whose name I can't recall, said that when he died he was going to 
ask God about turbulence.

I think that, with a little imagination, we can apply this quantitative,
correlative view of emergence to a host of other phenomena.  I strongly
recommend that anyone interested in emergence read _Order out of Chaos_, by
Ilya Prigogine & Isabelle Stengers, for a discussion of such behavior in
a variety of natural phenomena.

-- Simon

