At  1:28 PM 6/21/94 -0700, Eric Watt Forste wrote:
>>Neither neoclassical nor Austrian theories of money support this
>>conclusion. How would we test this statement?
>
>Austrian theory as developed by George Selgin supports it.

One of his proposals involved freezing the monetary base and continuing to
use dollars. I'm sure niether he nor White equates free banking with
commodity money. Mises's theory of money & credit suggests that the value
of money is based on expectations of future exchangeability, which must be
rooted in some previous connection to a commodity but need not maintain
that connection. All you need is some way to maintain those expectations.

[...]
> Money was understood to be merely a distinguished
>commodity until well after free banking was completely suppressed in
>the MDCs.

Post hoc ergo propter hoc. Monetary institutions were still primitive, as
was the public and scholarly understanding of them.

>>game is half-conceded?" What is the game, anonymity, fed bashing, or
>>inflation protection?
>
>It is no different, and the game is all of the above.

The extropian game is all the above, the digicash game is only anonymity.
>[...]
>I also doubt that the bureaucrats will give us financial privacy just
>because we ask nicely. Serial-number tracking technology, which would
>make every FRN a marked note, is not far in the future at all.

You can use $s as a unit to denominate your debits and credits without ever
using an actual serial numbered note yourself.

>>What unit of account will cyberspace adopt? What medium of redemption?
>
>These questions are unanswerable, because they require predicting the
>future. What we need to decide is what units and media *should*
>cyberspace adopt? We are not haplessly sitting around waiting for
>something to happen to us; *we* are the people who are going to be
>designing and implementing these systems. So the question is not
>"what will?" but "what should?".

There's a lot of positive feedback in money, so knowing the competitors
should give a pretty good indication of the future. Knowing what is likely
to happen without interference provides insight into how to get people to
accept what we want more easily. We can't ram it down their throats.
Standards can fail, implementations can stagnate.
>
>I think we have every reason to want to dump central-bank-controlled
>currencies, and (given the current regulatory state of affairs) few or
>no reasons for wanting to preserve them.

Once the central bank is gone, what's so odious about the currency?

[from another post]
>in all U. S. jurisdictions, you must get official
>permission from the state to open a new bank. Such permission is very
>difficult to get in most states. No such permission is required (in
>most states) to open a new barter exchange or commodity depository.
>So backing digital cash with anything of value other than fiat
>currency would expose one to far less bureaucratic oversight.

I find this reasoning much more persuasive. This even seems like a possible
path to separating the unit of account from the medium of exchange. But
doesn't the IRS keep a pretty enthusiastic eye on barter exchanges, and
aren't there regs on precious metals dealers, etc? What unit do barter
exchanges use for accounting?

